course: dictionary
lesson: 1997
title: Concede: admit it, give it up
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words tell you what someone does when they give in. Concede is one of them. It means to admit that something is true, often when you do not really want to. «Accepting that you lost a fair vote is called conceding.» The clue is accepting that you lost: the word shows giving in.
explain: Look at the words around it. In sport, a team that concedes a goal lets the other team score. In a debate, a speaker who concedes a point agrees that the other side has a fair argument. Try your meaning in the sentence: "Accepting that you lost is called giving in" still makes sense, so you are on the right track.
explain: «To concede is to accept that you lost; it shows supporters the election was fair and lets power pass peacefully to the winner.» Here the context tells you that conceding is a calm, fair thing to do after an election. Does admitting defeat fit the sentence? Yes, so you have the right meaning.
fact: Concede means to admit, often unwillingly, that something is true.
fact: To concede defeat means to admit you have lost.
fact: In sport, to concede a goal means to let the other team score.
fact: To concede a point means to agree that the other side has a good argument.
fact: Formally, concede can also mean to give something up or allow it.
exercise: «He conceded that I was right.» What does conceded mean here? | He shouted at me until I left | He forgot what I had said | *He admitted, unwillingly, that I was correct | He ran away from the argument
exercise: The losing team conceded a goal in the last minute. What does conceded a goal mean? | They scored a goal themselves | They gave the ball back | *They let the other team score | They stopped the game
exercise: «Accepting that you lost a fair vote is called conceding.» Find and copy the word that means admitting you have lost. | called | fair | vote | *conceding
test: In an election, the person who loses may concede. What does this mean? | They ask for a new vote with more people | They refuse to speak to anyone | *They admit they have lost | They win the election anyway
test: A debater says, "I concede that point." What does the debater do? | They laugh at the other side | They run out of the room | *They agree the other side has a fair point | They ask for the point to be repeated
exam: Which phrase means to admit you have lost? | concede a goal | concede something to the other team's advantage | *concede defeat | concede a point
